  "title": "Malicious user can DoS the withdrawal process of the GTL token.",
  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nOn short explanation GTL is a ERC4626 token and users deposit USDC in exchange for it, an important difference is that in order to withdraw or redeem users cannot use the regular ERC4626 functions like `withdraw` or `reedem`, but will need to queue a withdrawal which is later processed by the admin.\n\n```solidity\n    function previewWithdraw(uint256) public pure override returns (uint256 assets) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    function maxWithdraw(address) public pure override returns (uint256) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    function maxRedeem(address) public pure override returns (uint256) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n```\nKey points for the functions `queueWithdrawal`, `processWithdrawals` and `cancelWithdrawal`:\n- the system burns the shares when processing the actual withdrawal not when queueing.\n- you cannot process queue orders individually, the process starts from the earliest id in the queue list.\n- only the msg.sender which placed the queue withdrawal order can cancel it.\n\nAttack path for malicious user like Bob:\n1. Bob has a particular balance of shares, he queues a withdrawal.\n2. Afterwards Bob transfers his balance of shares to a second EOA.\n3. Processing the withdrawal reverts on burn as Bob has zero balance of shares.\n4. Processing withdrawals is bricked for everyone in the queue list.\n```solidity\n    function queueWithdrawal(uint256 shares) external returns (uint256 id) {\n        if (shares == 0) revert InsufficientWithdrawal();\n        if (_queuedShares[msg.sender] + shares > balanceOf(msg.sender)) revert InsufficientBalance();\n\n        id = ++_withdrawalCounter;\n\n        _queuedShares[msg.sender] += shares;\n        _queuedWithdrawal[id] = Withdrawal(msg.sender, shares);\n        _withdrawalQueue.push(id);\n\n        emit WithdrawalQueued(id, msg.sender, shares);\n    }\n```\n```solidity\n    function processWithdrawals(uint256 num) external onlyAdmin {\n        if (num > _withdrawalQueue.length) revert InsufficientWithdrawalsQueued();\n\n        uint256 allocatedAssets = orderbookCollateral() + freeCollateralBalance() + totalAccountValue();\n\n        uint256 id;\n        Withdrawal memory withdrawal;\n        uint256 assets;\n        for (uint256 i; i < num; ++i) {\n            id = _withdrawalQueue[i];\n            withdrawal = _queuedWithdrawal[id];\n\n            assets = _convertToAssets({shares: withdrawal.shares, allocatedAssets: allocatedAssets});\n\n            delete  _queuedWithdrawal[id];\n\n            _queuedShares[withdrawal.account] -= withdrawal.shares;\n\n            _burn(withdrawal.account, withdrawal.shares);\n\n            usdc.safeTransfer(withdrawal.account, assets);\n\n            emit WithdrawalProcessed(id, withdrawal.account, withdrawal.shares, assets);\n        }\n\n        _dequeueBatch(num);\n    }\n```\n```solidity\n    function cancelWithdrawal(uint256 id) external {\n        if (_queuedWithdrawal[id].account != msg.sender) revert NotPerpManager();\n\n        _queuedShares[msg.sender] -= _queuedWithdrawal[id].shares;\n        delete _queuedWithdrawal[id];\n\n        _dequeue(id);\n\n        emit WithdrawalCanceled(id);\n    }\n```\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nBurn the shares when queueing the withdrawal order in `queueWithdrawal`, mint the shares back incase of a canceling a queue order in `cancelWithdrawal`."
